msgctxt "AllHelp|"
msgid ""
"\n"
"\n"
"Advanced Preferences\n"
"=========\n"
"\n"
"With the advanced parameters in JASP you can specify the following options:\n"
"(All these settings will remain after restarting JASP.)\n"
"\n"
"## Modules options\n"
"\n"
"### Remember enabled modules\n"
"If you've enabled this option then JASP will remember which modules are "
"activated and make sure they remain that way even when you close JASP. So "
"supposing `Summary Statistics` was enabled and JASP closes then after "
"reopening JASP it will be enabled immediately.\n"
"\n"
"### Developer mode\n"
"\n"
"If you enable this you see a few extra options appear. One is \"Generate "
"markdown files for help\", which will ignore any markdown helpfiles for an "
"analysis and instead will only show generated markdown from each `info` "
"field on each qml item and jaspObject. Another option is the \"Module "
"library URL\" field, which allows you to change the URL of the JASP module "
"library web application from [https://module-library.jasp-stats.org/]"
"(https://module-library.jasp-stats.org/) to a locally running instance.\n"
"\n"
"#### Development module\n"
"Besides that it will show \"Development module\" where you can load an R-"
"package installed/restored with `renv`. For this you need to know the name "
"of your module (for instance: `jaspAnova`) and the R-library or `.libPath` "
"that `renv` created for you. \n"
"\n"
"To use `renv` to install your package you first `git clone` or `git "
"checkout` the code you want.\n"
"An example using `jaspAnova`:\n"
"```bash\n"
"# in a terminal:\n"
"git clone https://github.com/jasp-stats/jaspAnova\n"
"cd jaspAnova\n"
"R\n"
"```\n"
"If you don't have `renv` installed already you can get it by running "
"`install.packages('renv')`.\n"
"Thus we are in the `jaspAnova` folder we just created and then in R:\n"
"```R\n"
"\n"
"renv::restore(clean = TRUE)     # Project library is filled with renv.lock "
"dependencies\n"
"renv::install('.')              # Install local pkg to project library\n"
"\n"
"message(\"R Project library for developer mode:\\n\", .libPaths()[1])\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"This will print the project library you need for running your module. \n"
"You make sure to copy that to the required preference field and do the same "
"for the module name.\n"
"\n"
"As a sidenote: running the first `renv` command for a project library might "
"actually trigger `renv::init`, which will ask the user to agree to managing "
"some files for the user. Just answer `Y` there.\n"
"\n"
"#### Temporary project library\n"
"Often the default library is also the user's normal R library, for instance "
"something like \"C:/Users/A_User/AppData/Local/R/win-library/4.5\". "
"Restoring with `renv` there can mess up the entire normal library then, as "
"it removes and adds packages based on the development module being "
"installed. While this is very fast and often avoids reinstalling stuff it "
"might be undesirable.\n"
"A simple way to avoid this is to tell `renv` to install to a temporary "
"folder, keeping your usual library as-is.\n"
"```R\n"
".libPaths(tempdir())\n"
"# restore, install and print project library\n"
"```\n"
"On Windows you would then get something like: `C:/Users/A_User/AppData/Local/"
"Temp/RtmpYN3aqC`\n"
"\n"
"#### Dedicated project library\n"
"You could of course also decide to make a dedicated project library "
"somewhere and use that instead of `tempdir()`, or if the temporary directory "
"it creates isn't accessible for JASP. \n"
"As an example, you can create a directory somewhere and pass that to "
"`renv`.\n"
"```R\n"
".libPaths(\"~/jasp-renv-package-library\")\n"
"```\n"
"This will load `jasp-renv-package-library` from your homefolder (which would "
"make it `/home/username/jasp-renv-package-library` on macos/linux or `c:"
"\\Users\\username\\jasp-renv-package-library` on windows).\n"
"You then do the above `renv::restore` and `renv::install`.\n"
"\n"
"#### Installing extra packages\n"
"Until we finish syntaxmode for JASP, which is now in beta, you might want to "
"use `jaspTools` to run analyses in R. You can just run "
"`renv::install('jaspTools') and it will be installed to your project "
"library. The same goes for any other package(s) you might need.\n"
"\n"
"If you've installed other packages while developing you might want to add "
"them to the `renv.lock` file, is easy. However you probably don't want to "
"add all the packages loaded to the `renv.lock` file, so we run it while "
"excluding some packages:\n"
"```\n"
"renv::snapshot(exclude=c('jaspTools'))\n"
"```\n"
"If there are other packages you do not want listed as a dependency you can "
"simply add them to the `exclude` list, but `jaspTools` should definitely not "
"be a dependency of a jaspModule.\n"
"\n"
"#### renv guide\n"
"To understand more about `renv` you should consider reading their [getting "
"started guide](https://rstudio.github.io/renv/articles/renv.html). It will "
"explain what a project library is, and why this system is helpful in the "
"first place.\n"
"\n"
"### Configuration file options\n"
"\n"
"Here you can select a path to a `toml` configuration file for JASP.\n"
"This allows you to preload certain modules on a system, or set some default "
"options for particular analyses. This file can be located on a users "
"computer or come from a remote URL for workplace deployment.\n"
"\n"
"<details>\n"
"<summary>Creating you own configuration file</summary>\n"
"\n"
"To make your own configuration file you create a `toml` file somewhere, for "
"instance `my-first-jaspconfig.toml`. An example:\n"
"```toml\n"
"Format = \"0.1.0\"\n"
"JASPVersion = \"0.19.3\"\n"
"\n"
"EnabledModules = [\"jaspAudit\",]\n"
"\n"
"[Constants]\n"
"rain = true\n"
"\n"
"[Modules.jaspAudit.Constants]\n"
"high = 90\n"
"medium = 50\n"
"low = 40\n"
"\n"
"[Modules.jaspAudit.Analyses.auditClassicalWorkflow.Constants]\n"
"high = 77 #these will take precedent over the Module wide and JASP global "
"constants\n"
"low = 42\n"
"\n"
"[Modules.jaspAudit.Analyses.auditClassicalWorkflow.Options]\n"
"min_precision_test = {Value = true, Lock = true} #comment\n"
"materiality_test = false #comment\n"
"min_precision_rel_val = 0.14\n"
"materiality_rel_val = {Value = 0.02, Lock= true}\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"These constants can be used by module developers to change some settings for "
"particular users and incorporate that smoothly into their analysisforms (qml "
"files).\n"
"They can be accessed as follows:\n"
"```qml\n"
"x = form.getConstant(\"constant\", <default value if not defined>)\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"</details>\n"
"\n"
"### Github personal access tokens\n"
"When a dynamic module is installed JASP uses R internally to get all "
"dependencies and for this it requests info from https://github.com and often "
"this goes well.\n"
"They do have a rate limiter there however, see https://docs.github.com/en/"
"rest/overview/resources-in-the-rest-api#rate-limiting and that can cause the "
"module installation to fail.\n"
"\n"
"To work around this we have added some options to manage a so called "
"personal access token, and it is then passed on to R through an environment "
"variable called `GITHUB_PAT`. This is also very useful for doing stuff in "
"RStudio.\n"
"\n"
"We added a default one that is available for R inside of JASP, but this is "
"shared with all JASP users and thus might (at some point) become "
"oversaturated. In that case you might want to add your own.\n"
"\n"
"This can be done by generating a new token for your github account at "
"https://github.com/settings/tokens/new and then either copying the resulting "
"code into the settings here in JASP or through your OS. If set in your OS, "
"you might have done this for R already, JASP will automatically use it if "
"set use \"the default\" value.\n"
"\n"
"Please *DO NOT* give this personal access token **any permissions** at all, "
"because it really isn't necessary for the way it is used in JASP.\n"
"\n"
"Otherwise you can set it specifically in JASP by unchecking \"Use default "
"PAT\" and then copying your token-code into the custom GITHUB_PAT textbox, "
"this is probably the easiest if you do not know what an environment variable "
"is.\n"
"\n"
"## Logging options\n"
"\n"
"### Log to file\n"
"When you check this JASP will start logging many of the actions it performs "
"to logfiles. \n"
"Logging is especially useful when you are developing your own module, or run "
"into a problem and wish to reach out to the development team.\n"
"The logs might help us give insight in the nature of your problem. Note that "
"you might need to restart JASP for the logging process to start.\n"
"The number in the input field \"Max logfiles to keep\" defines how many "
"logfiles will be kept at maximum to conserve diskspace. Any extra, older, "
"logfiles will be removed.\n"
"The files can be viewed by pressing \"Show logs\".\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"## Engine options\n"
"JASP uses R in the background to run all the analyses. Here you can change "
"how many JASP is allowed to start at the most. When deciding how many "
"maximum engines you might want it is helpful to keep in mind that right now "
"each module is ran by only a single engine at the moment. So having 10 "
"analyses from the ANOVA module will not go any faster by allowing maximally "
"10 engines. While running 10 analyses from 10 modules will go *way* faster.\n"
"\n"
"### Sandbox\n"
"On Windows you will also see the \"Sandbox engine\" checkbox. When enabled "
"this will isolate the engine process in such a way that none of the code we "
"run in R is able to do anything with the rest of your system. No networking "
"nor access to files on the system.\n"
"This is good, because we rely on a lot of opensource third party R packages "
"from CRAN and Github. And while we have not heard of any supplychain attacks "
"via the R package ecosystem we think it is better to avoid such problems as "
"much as possible.\n"
"\n"
"When developing R modules (and thus enabling \"Developer mode\") this will "
"be disabled by default because otherwise the engine won't be able to load "
"your development module.\n"
"\n"
"On macOs the operating system already implements at least a file access "
"sandbox for applications and will ask for permission before accessing files "
"outside it's \"JASP.app\". \n"
"\n"
"The same goes for the flatpak distribution of JASP on Linux, which in "
"essence is a sandbox, which mediates access through file dialogs and the "
"like. Here however we ask for home-directory read-access so that we can get "
"normal user readable paths to recent files.\n"
"This is entirely disableable by changing JASP's configuration per `flatpak "
"override org.jaspstats.JASP --nofilesystem=~`.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"### Show engines\n"
"This opens a window the developers use to keep an eye on what each engine is "
"up top.\n"
"It could be interesting to see what engines are busy and with what, although "
"the level isn't very detailed. It does however give you the option to "
"manually kill and engine by right-clicking it. \n"
"This can be helpful if it stopped responding during some particularly heavy "
"yet unnecessary calculation.\n"
msgstr ""
"\n\nAdvanced Preferences\n=========\n\nWith the advanced parameters in"
"JASP you can specify the following options:\n(All these settings will"
"remain after restarting JASP.)\n\n## Modules options\n\n### Remember"
"enabled modules\nIf you've enabled this option then JASP will remember"
"which modules are activated and make sure they remain that way even when"
"you close JASP. So supposing `Summary Statistics` was enabled and JASP"
"closes then after reopening JASP it will be enabled immediately.\n\n###"
"Developer mode\n\nIf you enable this you see a few extra options appear."
"One is \"Generate markdown files for help\", which will ignore any markdown"
"helpfiles for an analysis and instead will only show generated markdown"
"from each `info` field on each qml item and jaspObject. Another option is"
"the \"Module library URL\" field, which allows you to change the URL of the"
"JASP module library web application from [https://module-library.jasp-stats"
".org/](https://module-library.jasp-stats.org/) to a locally running"
"instance.\n\n#### Development module\nBesides that it will show"
"\"Development module\" where you can load an R-package installed/restored"
"with `renv`. For this you need to know the name of your module (for"
"instance: `jaspAnova`) and the R-library or `.libPath` that `renv` created"
"for you. \n\nTo use `renv` to install your package you first `git clone`"
"or `git checkout` the code you want.\nAn example using"
"`jaspAnova`:\n```bash\n# in a terminal:\ngit clone"
"https://github.com/jasp-stats/jaspAnova\ncd jaspAnova\nR\n```\nIf you"
"don't have `renv` installed already you can get it by running"
"`install.packages('renv')`.\nThus we are in the `jaspAnova` folder we just"
"created and then in R:\n```R\n\nrenv::restore(clean = TRUE)     #"
"Project library is filled with renv.lock dependencies\nrenv::install('.') "
"# Install local pkg to project library\n\nmessage(\"R Project library for"
"developer mode:\n\", .libPaths()[1])\n```\n\nThis will print the"
"project library you need for running your module. \nYou make sure to copy"
"that to the required preference field and do the same for the module"
"name.\n\nAs a sidenote: running the first `renv` command for a project"
"library might actually trigger `renv::init`, which will ask the user to"
"agree to managing some files for the user. Just answer `Y` there.\n\n####"
"Temporary project library\nOften the default library is also the user's"
"normal R library, for instance something like"
"\"C:/Users/A_User/AppData/Local/R/win-library/4.5\". Restoring with `renv`"
"there can mess up the entire normal library then, as it removes and adds"
"packages based on the development module being installed. While this is"
"very fast and often avoids reinstalling stuff it might be undesirable.\nA"
"simple way to avoid this is to tell `renv` to install to a temporary"
"folder, keeping your usual library as-is.\n```R\n.libPaths(tempdir())\n#"
"restore, install and print project library\n```\nOn Windows you would"
"then get something like: `C:/Users/A_User/AppData/Local/Temp/RtmpYN3aqC`\n"
"\n#### Dedicated project library\nYou could of course also decide to make"
"a dedicated project library somewhere and use that instead of `tempdir()`,"
"or if the temporary directory it creates isn't accessible for JASP. \nAs"
"an example, you can create a directory somewhere and pass that to"
"`renv`.\n```R\n.libPaths(\"~/jasp-renv-package-library\")\n```\nThis"
"will load `jasp-renv-package-library` from your homefolder (which would"
"make it `/home/username/jasp-renv-package-library` on macos/linux or"
"`c:\Users\username\jasp-renv-package-library` on windows).\nYou then do"
"the above `renv::restore` and `renv::install`.\n\n#### Installing extra"
"packages\nUntil we finish syntaxmode for JASP, which is now in beta, you"
"might want to use `jaspTools` to run analyses in R. You can just run"
"`renv::install('jaspTools') and it will be installed to your project"
"library. The same goes for any other package(s) you might need.\n\nIf"
"you've installed other packages while developing you might want to add them"
"to the `renv.lock` file, is easy. However you probably don't want to add"
"all the packages loaded to the `renv.lock` file, so we run it while"
"excluding some packages:\n```\nrenv::snapshot(exclude=c('jaspTools'))\n`"
"``\nIf there are other packages you do not want listed as a dependency you"
"can simply add them to the `exclude` list, but `jaspTools` should"
"definitely not be a dependency of a jaspModule.\n\n#### renv guide\nTo"
"understand more about `renv` you should consider reading their [getting"
"started guide](https://rstudio.github.io/renv/articles/renv.html). It will"
"explain what a project library is, and why this system is helpful in the"
"first place.\n\n### Configuration file options\n\nHere you can select a"
"path to a `toml` configuration file for JASP.\nThis allows you to preload"
"certain modules on a system, or set some default options for particular"
"analyses. This file can be located on a users computer or come from a"
"remote URL for workplace deployment.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Creating you"
"own configuration file</summary>\n\nTo make your own configuration file"
"you create a `toml` file somewhere, for instance"
"`my-first-jaspconfig.toml`. An example:\n```toml\nFormat ="
"\"0.1.0\"\nJASPVersion = \"0.19.3\"\n\nEnabledModules ="
"[\"jaspAudit\",]\n\n[Constants]\nrain ="
"true\n\n[Modules.jaspAudit.Constants]\nhigh = 90\nmedium = 50\nlow ="
"40\n\n[Modules.jaspAudit.Analyses.auditClassicalWorkflow.Constants]\nhig"
"h = 77 #these will take precedent over the Module wide and JASP global"
"constants\nlow = 42\n\n[Modules.jaspAudit.Analyses.auditClassicalWorkflo"
"w.Options]\nmin_precision_test = {Value = true, Lock = true}"
"#comment\nmateriality_test = false #comment\nmin_precision_rel_val ="
"0.14\nmateriality_rel_val = {Value = 0.02, Lock= true}\n```\n\nThese"
"constants can be used by module developers to change some settings for"
"particular users and incorporate that smoothly into their analysisforms"
"(qml files).\nThey can be accessed as follows:\n```qml\nx ="
"form.getConstant(\"constant\", <default value if not"
"defined>)\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### Github personal access"
"tokens\nWhen a dynamic module is installed JASP uses R internally to get"
"all dependencies and for this it requests info from https://github.com and"
"often this goes well.\nThey do have a rate limiter there however, see"
"https://docs.github.com/en/rest/overview/resources-in-the-rest-api#rate-lim"
"iting and that can cause the module installation to fail.\n\nTo work"
"around this we have added some options to manage a so called personal"
"access token, and it is then passed on to R through an environment variable"
"called `GITHUB_PAT`. This is also very useful for doing stuff in"
"RStudio.\n\nWe added a default one that is available for R inside of"
"JASP, but this is shared with all JASP users and thus might (at some point)"
"become oversaturated. In that case you might want to add your"
"own.\n\nThis can be done by generating a new token for your github"
"account at https://github.com/settings/tokens/new and then either copying"
"the resulting code into the settings here in JASP or through your OS. If"
"set in your OS, you might have done this for R already, JASP will"
"automatically use it if set use \"the default\" value.\n\nPlease *DO NOT*"
"give this personal access token **any permissions** at all, because it"
"really isn't necessary for the way it is used in JASP.\n\nOtherwise you"
"can set it specifically in JASP by unchecking \"Use default PAT\" and then"
"copying your token-code into the custom GITHUB_PAT textbox, this is"
"probably the easiest if you do not know what an environment variable"
"is.\n\n## Logging options\n\n### Log to file\nWhen you check this JASP"
"will start logging many of the actions it performs to logfiles. \nLogging"
"is especially useful when you are developing your own module, or run into a"
"problem and wish to reach out to the development team.\nThe logs might"
"help us give insight in the nature of your problem. Note that you might"
"need to restart JASP for the logging process to start.\nThe number in the"
"input field \"Max logfiles to keep\" defines how many logfiles will be kept"
"at maximum to conserve diskspace. Any extra, older, logfiles will be"
"removed.\nThe files can be viewed by pressing \"Show logs\".\n\n\n##"
"Engine options\nJASP uses R in the background to run all the analyses."
"Here you can change how many JASP is allowed to start at the most. When"
"deciding how many maximum engines you might want it is helpful to keep in"
"mind that right now each module is ran by only a single engine at the"
"moment. So having 10 analyses from the ANOVA module will not go any faster"
"by allowing maximally 10 engines. While running 10 analyses from 10 modules"
"will go *way* faster.\n\n### Sandbox\nOn Windows you will also see the"
"\"Sandbox engine\" checkbox. When enabled this will isolate the engine"
"process in such a way that none of the code we run in R is able to do"
"anything with the rest of your system. No networking nor access to files on"
"the system.\nThis is good, because we rely on a lot of opensource third"
"party R packages from CRAN and Github. And while we have not heard of any"
"supplychain attacks via the R package ecosystem we think it is better to"
"avoid such problems as much as possible.\n\nWhen developing R modules"
"(and thus enabling \"Developer mode\") this will be disabled by default"
"because otherwise the engine won't be able to load your development"
"module.\n\nOn macOs the operating system already implements at least a"
"file access sandbox for applications and will ask for permission before"
"accessing files outside it's \"JASP.app\". \n\nThe same goes for the"
"flatpak distribution of JASP on Linux, which in essence is a sandbox, which"
"mediates access through file dialogs and the like. Here however we ask for"
"home-directory read-access so that we can get normal user readable paths to"
"recent files.\nThis is entirely disableable by changing JASP's"
"configuration per `flatpak override org.jaspstats.JASP"
"--nofilesystem=~`.\n\n\n### Show engines\nThis opens a window the"
"developers use to keep an eye on what each engine is up top.\nIt could be"
"interesting to see what engines are busy and with what, although the level"
"isn't very detailed. It does however give you the option to manually kill"
"and engine by right-clicking it. \nThis can be helpful if it stopped"
"responding during some particularly heavy yet unnecessary calculation.\n"

msgctxt "AllHelp|"
msgid ""
"\n"
"\n"
"Data Preferences\n"
"=========\n"
"\n"
"Regarding data handling in JASP you can choose the following options:\n"
"(All these settings will remain after restarting JASP.)\n"
"\n"
"### Synchronize automatically on data file save\n"
"\n"
"If you change a loaded data file in JASP from outside JASP\n"
"(e.g., in your preferred editor, see below), this checkbox determines\n"
"if results in JASP are automatically synchronized or not.\n"
"You can also start the synchronization manually from the main menu\n"
"or using keyboard shortcuts:\n"
"\n"
"- OSX: &#8984; with Y\n"
"- Windows and Linux: Ctrl with Y\n"
"\n"
"### Use default spreadsheet editor\n"
"\n"
"In JASP you can open the datafile by double clicking the data pane.\n"
"This opens your data file in your preferred editor which you can specify "
"here\n"
"or the default editor chosen by your operating system.\n"
"\n"
"### Import settings\n"
"\n"
"#### Order by values\n"
"\n"
"This will make sure labels are always sorted by their value on import and "
"synchronisation.\n"
"It does however incur a slowdown that is usually negligible, but for certain "
"cases quite a large one.\n"
"This would be the case for large, >500k rows of different scalar values (for "
"instance, random ones).\n"
"In such a scenario you might consider disabling this, but for most people "
"the default *on* setting is fine.\n"
"\n"
"#### Threshold for Scale\n"
"\n"
"Importing data in JASP has a threshold value that determines if a column "
"should be treated\n"
"as a Scale type or as a Categorical (Nominal or Ordinal) type. The default "
"value of this parameter is 10.\n"
"This means that if you have fewer (or equal) than 10 different integers in "
"the data, the column\n"
"gets the Ordinal type (Nominal type if only 2 different integers are found) "
"else it will get the Scale type. Be aware that this value is used when\n"
"importing the data, so data needs to be reloaded (or synchronized) to take "
"effect.\n"
"\n"
"#### Maximum allowed levels for scale when used as nominal/ordinal\n"
"\n"
"Analyses may specify that some variables should be nominal or ordinal. But a "
"scale variable might be used and interpreted as nominal or ordinal. However "
"many scale variables cannot really be interpreted as nominal or ordinal and "
"will generate too many levels, that most analyses cannot accept.\n"
"To prevent this, this setting specifies what is the maximum number of levels "
"that will be accepted for a scale value to be interpretable as a nominal or "
"ordinal.<br>\n"
"This setting is not used if an analysis specifies explicitly a maximum "
"number of levels: i.e. for 'Grouping Variables' in Independent Samples T-"
"Test, the maximun (and minimum) of levels of a variables is set to 2.<br>\n"
"But for most analyses this default setting is used: this prevent the user "
"from using wrong variables, and make the engine running too long.\n"
"\n"
"### Show missing values as\n"
"\n"
"JASP shows missing values as blank in cells by default, you can also label "
"them as others (e.g., it can even be defined as \"😀\" or other characters) "
"in text field to display friendly on data pane. Note that this is different "
"from a valid value label and means it won't appear in the results.\n"
"\n"
"### Missing Value List\n"
"\n"
"In the Missing Value list you can specify when observations in your datafile "
"should be treated as missing (e.g., if you coded missing observations to be "
"999, you can add this value here and JASP will treat all cells with the "
"value 999 as missing).\n"
"You can set here the default values that will be used for a new file. If you "
"load a JASP file, it will load also the missing values used by this file: "
"this values can be seen in the 'Workspace missing values' in the Data "
"editing mode.\n"
"It is also possible to set custom missing values per column (by double-"
"clicking the header of a column).\n"
"You can delete values from this list by selecting them and pressing the "
"minus button.\n"
"Clicking on \"Reset\" will restore this list with the JASP default values.\n"
"\n"
"### Windows workaround\n"
"\n"
"Option here specifically for importing CSV files on Windows. JASP usually "
"try to import `.csv` data file in UTF encoding and/or with a BOM.\n"
"If someone saves a CSV from excel in their \"local codepage\" however there "
"is no BOM and there is no way to determine what the codepage is from the "
"CSV.\n"
"Because JASP now runs exclusively in UTF-8 it can't just use the system "
"codepage, and in any case this wouldnt help if you got the file from someone "
"else (working in a different locale/codepage).\n"
"Without this workaround JASP just assumes it is in some kind of UTF variant, "
"which tends to be true and also is the most interoperable way of sharing "
"files between nations.\n"
"But to help microsoft office users a dropdown has been provided where the "
"expected codepage can be chosen and if the workaround is turned on it will "
"be used to decode such CSV's.\n"
msgstr ""
"\n\nPreferenze dati\n=========\n\nRper quanto riguarda la gestione dei"
"dati in JASP è possibile scegliere le seguenti opzioni:\n(Tutte queste"
"impostazioni rimarranno dopo il riavvio JASP.)\n\n### Sincronizza"
"automaticamente al salvataggio del file di dati\n\nSe si modifica un file"
"di dati caricato in JASP dall'esterno JASP\n(ad esempio, nel tuo editor"
"preferito, vedi sotto), questa casella di controllo determina\nse i"
"risultati in JASP vengono sincronizzati automaticamente o meno.\nPuoi"
"anche avviare la sincronizzazione manualmente dal menu principale\né"
"utilizzando le scorciatoie da tastiera:\n\n- OSX: &#8984; con Y\n-"
"Windows e Linux: Ctrl con Y\n\n### Usa predefinito editor di fogli di"
"calcolo\n\nIn JASP puoi aprire il file di dati facendo doppio clic sul"
"riquadro dei dati.\nQuesto apre il file di dati nell'editor preferito che"
"puoi specificare qui\né nell'editor predefinito scelto dal tuo sistema"
"operativo.\n\n### Impostazioni di importazione\n\n#### Ordina per"
"valori\n\nCiò assicurerà che le etichette siano sempre ordinate in base"
"al loro valore durante l'importazione e la sincronizzazione.\nTuttavia"
"comporta un rallentamento che di solito è trascurabile, ma per certi casi"
"piuttosto grandi.\nQuesto sarebbe il caso di righe grandi, >500.000 con"
"valori scalari diversi (ad esempio, casuali).\nIn uno scenario del genere"
"potresti considerare di disabilitarlo, ma per la maggior parte delle"
"persone l'impostazione predefinita *on* va bene.\n\n#### Soglia per"
"scala\n\nL'importazione dei dati in JASP ha un valore di soglia che"
"determina se una colonna deve essere trattata\come tipo di scala o come"
"categoriale Tipo (Nominale o Ordinale). Il valore predefinito di questo"
"parametro è 10.\nCiò significa che se nei dati sono presenti meno (o"
"uguali) di 10 numeri interi diversi, la colonna\ngetterà il tipo Ordinale"
"(tipo Nominale se vengono trovati solo 2 numeri interi diversi), altrimenti"
"otterrà il tipo Scala. Tieni presente che questo valore viene utilizzato"
"durante\nl'importazione dei dati, quindi i dati devono essere ricaricati"
"(o sincronizzati) per avere effetto.\n\n#### Livelli massimi consentiti"
"per la scala se utilizzati come nominali/ordinali\n\nLe analisi possono"
"specificare che alcune variabili devono essere nominali o ordinali. Ma una"
"variabile di scala potrebbe essere utilizzata e interpretata come nominale"
"o ordinale. Tuttavia, molte variabili di scala non possono essere realmente"
"interpretate come nominali o ordinali e genereranno troppi livelli che la"
"maggior parte delle analisi non possono accettare.\nPer evitare ciò,"
"questa impostazione specifica qual è il numero massimo di livelli che sarà"
"accettato affinché un valore di scala sia interpretabile come nominale o"
"ordinale.<br>\nQuesta impostazione non viene utilizzata se un'analisi"
"specifica esplicitamente un numero massimo di livelli: ad esempio per il"
"\"Raggruppamento delle variabili\" nel T-Test dei campioni indipendenti, il"
"massimo (e il minimo) di i livelli di una variabile sono impostati su"
"2.<br>\nMa per la maggior parte delle analisi viene utilizzata questa"
"impostazione predefinita: ciò impedisce all'utente di utilizzare variabili"
"errate e fa funzionare il motore troppo a lungo.\n\n### Mostra i valori"
"mancanti come\n\nJASP mostra i valori mancanti come vuoti nelle celle per"
"impostazione predefinita, puoi anche etichettarli come altri (ad esempio,"
"può anche essere definito come \"😀\" o altri caratteri) nel campo di testo"
"per visualizzare in modo amichevole i dati riquadro. Tieni presente che"
"questo è diverso da un'etichetta di valore valido e significa che non verrà"
"visualizzato nei risultati.\n\n### Elenco valori"
"mancanti\n\nNell'elenco Valori mancanti puoi specificare quando le"
"osservazioni nel tuo file di dati devono essere trattate come mancanti (ad"
"esempio, se hai codificato le osservazioni mancanti come 999, puoi"
"aggiungere questo valore qui e JASP tratterà tutte le celle con il valore"
"999 come mancanti).\nPuoi impostare qui i valori predefiniti che verranno"
"essere utilizzato per un nuovo file. Se carichi un file JASP, verranno"
"caricati anche i valori mancanti utilizzati da questo file: questi valori"
"possono essere visualizzati in 'Valori mancanti dell'area di lavoro' nella"
"modalità di modifica dei dati.\nÈ anche possibile impostare valori"
"mancanti personalizzati per colonna (facendo doppio clic sull'intestazione"
"di una colonna).\nÈ possibile eliminare i valori da questo elenco"
"selezionandoli e premendo il pulsante meno.\nFacendo clic su \"Reset\" si"
"ripristinerà questo elenco con il JASP valori predefiniti.\n\n### Windows"
"soluzione alternativa\n\nOpzione qui specifica per importare file CSV su"
"Windows. JASP di solito prova a importare il file di dati `.csv` in"
"codifica UTF e/o con una distinta base.\nSe qualcuno salva un CSV da Excel"
"nella propria \"codepage locale\", tuttavia non esiste una distinta base e"
"non c'è modo di determinare quale sia la codepage da CSV.\nPoiché JASP ora"
"viene eseguito esclusivamente in UTF-8, non può semplicemente utilizzare il"
"sistema codepage e in ogni caso questo non sarebbe d'aiuto se ricevessi il"
"file da qualcun altro (che lavora in una diversa impostazione"
"locale/codepage).\nSenza questa soluzione alternativa JASP presuppone"
"semplicemente che sia in una sorta di variante UTF, che tende ad essere"
"vera ed è anche il modo più interoperabile di condividere file tra"
"nazioni.\nMa per aiutare gli utenti di Microsoft Office è stato fornito un"
"menu a discesa in cui è possibile scegliere la codepage prevista e se la"
"soluzione alternativa è attivata verrà utilizzata per decodificarla CSV.\n"

msgctxt "AllHelp|"
msgid ""
"\n"
"\n"
"Results Preferences\n"
"=========\n"
"\n"
"Regarding analysis results in JASP you can choose the following options:\n"
"(All these settings will remain after restarting JASP.)\n"
"\n"
"## Table options\n"
"\n"
"### Display exact p-values\n"
"\n"
"Here you can specify if p-values should be shown as \"< .001\" or if an "
"exact value should be given.\n"
"\n"
"### Use exponent notation\n"
"\n"
"Here you can specify if scientific numbers should be displayed with exponent "
"notation as in 1e-10, instead of normalized like 1×10<sup>-10</sup>.\n"
"\n"
"### Fix the number of decimals\n"
"\n"
"This represents the number of decimals JASP will show for numeric values.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"## Plot options\n"
"\n"
"### Use PPI (Pixels Per Inch) of screen in plots\n"
"\n"
"Here you can specify the number of pixels per inch your plots should have. "
"By default it uses the value derived from your screen. This value can be "
"increased to create higher quality images.\n"
"\n"
"### Image background colour\n"
"\n"
"This option let's you toggle the background colour of plots between white "
"and transparant. You may find this especially useful when copying and/or "
"pasting plots.\n"
"\n"
"## Miscellaneous options\n"
"\n"
"### Show R syntax\n"
"\n"
"Show R syntax required to call each analysis in R.\n"
"\n"
"### Store analysis state and plot in jasp-files\n"
"\n"
"When an analysis runs it creates temporary files that contain the state of "
"the analysis and the same for each plot. \n"
"The analysis uses this to avoid rerunning the same code over and over again "
"by reusing old results whenever it can.\n"
"Enabling this option will allow you to store this in your jaspfile, so that "
"the next time you load it with this version of jasp you can rerun much "
"quicker. \n"
"However, your filesize might increase, sometimes by a lot.\n"
"\n"
"You might want to enable this option if you are working with analyses that "
"can take a long time for certain steps, like bootstrapping with a large N.\n"
"In that case storing it might save you a lot of time if you've closed JASP "
"in the meantime.\n"
"We've turned it off by default because in most cases the time it takes to "
"rerun it once is not so bad and it makes all jaspfiles smaller.\n"
"And when loading a jaspfile made by an older version this data is discarded "
"anyway.\n"
msgstr ""
"\n\nRPreferenze risultati\n=========\n\nRper quanto riguarda i"
"risultati dell'analisi in JASP puoi scegliere le seguenti opzioni:\n(Tutte"
"queste impostazioni rimarranno dopo il riavvio JASP.)\n\n## Opzioni"
"tabella\n\n### Visualizza valori p esatti\n\nQui puoi specificare se i"
"valori p devono essere mostrati come \"< .001\" or if an exact value should"
"be given.\n\n### Use exponent notation\n\nHere you can specify if"
"scientific numbers should be displayed with exponent notation as in 1e-10,"
"instead of normalized like 1×10<sup>-10</sup>.\n\n### Correggi il numero"
"di decimali\n\nQuesto rappresenta il numero di decimali che JASP mostrerà"
"per i valori numerici.\n\n\n## Opzioni di stampa\n\n### Usa PPI (pixel"
"per pollice) dello schermo nei grafici\n\nQui puoi specificare il numero"
"di pixel per pollice che i tuoi grafici dovrebbero avere. Per impostazione"
"predefinita è utilizza il valore derivato dallo schermo. Questo valore può"
"essere aumentato per creare immagini di qualità superiore.\n\n### Colore"
"di sfondo dell'immagine\n\nQuesta opzione ti consente di alternare il"
"colore di sfondo dei grafici tra bianco e trasparente. Potresti trovarlo"
"particolarmente utile quando copi e/o incolli i grafici.\n\n## Opzioni"
"varie\n\n### Mostra la sintassi R\n\nMostra la sintassi R richiesta"
"per. chiama ciascuna analisi in R.\n\n### Memorizza lo stato dell'analisi"
"e il grafico nei file jasp\n\nQuando viene eseguita un'analisi, crea file"
"temporanei che contengono lo stato dell'analisi e lo stesso per ogni"
"grafico \nL'analisi lo utilizza per evitare di rieseguire lo stesso codice"
"più e più volte riutilizzando i vecchi risultati ogni volta che"
"può.\nAbilitare questa opzione ti consentirà di memorizzarlo nel tuo file"
"jasp, in modo che la prossima volta che lo caricherai con questa versione"
"di jasp. può essere eseguito di nuovo molto più velocemente. \nTuttavia,"
"la dimensione del file potrebbe aumentare, a volte di molto.\n\nPotresti"
"voler abilitare questa opzione se stai lavorando con analisi che possono"
"richiedere molto tempo per determinati passaggi, come il bootstrap con un N"
"grande.\nIn tal caso, memorizzarlo potrebbe farti risparmiare molto tempo"
"se nel frattempo hai chiuso JASP.\nL'abbiamo disattivato per impostazione"
"predefinita perché nella maggior parte dei casi il tempo necessario per"
"rieseguirlo una volta non è così elevato e riduce tutti i file jasp.\nE"
"quando si carica un file jasp creato da una versione precedente questi dati"
"vengono comunque scartati.\n"

msgctxt "AllHelp|"
msgid ""
"\n"
"\n"
"User Interface\n"
"=========\n"
"\n"
"With the user interface parameters in JASP you can specify the following "
"options:\n"
"(All these settings will remain after restarting JASP.)\n"
"\n"
"### Fonts\n"
"Here you can specify which font will be used by the interface (File Menu, "
"Ribbon bar, Analysis options panel), the R, Lavaan or JAGS Code, or the "
"results. If no choice is made, the default font is used.\n"
"\n"
"Use Qt's textrendering: JASP using Qt to render interface text by default. "
"JASP will use native text rendering when the option unchecked. Try to check/"
"uncheck this if you find that the text on JASP looks terrible(blurry or "
"fuzzy...). This will take effect after restarting JASP.\n"
"\n"
"### Themes\n"
"Here you can specify if you would like to have a \"light\" or a \"dark\" "
"theme on the interface of JASP. The dark theme is much easier on the eyes if "
"you are in a dark environment while the light (and default) theme is clearer "
"in bright light.\n"
"\n"
"### Preferred Language\n"
"Select the language in which you would like to use JASP.\n"
"\n"
"If you would like to improve our translations, or to add a new language, "
"please visit https://jasp-stats.org/translation-guidelines/\n"
"\n"
"### Alternative Locale\n"
"This is by default on, and enables a selection menu of languages and "
"territories. \n"
"Whatever you select here is what will determine how your numbers in JASP are "
"formatted, such as but not limited to, whether the decimal separator is a "
"',' or a '.' for instance.\n"
"It will also be used to format monetary columns in the output, and in the "
"future also date, time and datetime columns. \n"
"This will also determine what kind of thousands-separators are used.\n"
"If you disable using an alternative locale it will use the locale "
"corresponding to the selected language.\n"
"\n"
"### ALT-Navigation mode\n"
"Turn the ALT activated navigation mode on or off.\n"
"\n"
"### Check for updates\n"
"JASP doesn't share any of your data when it gets updates, not even which "
"version of JASP you are using.\n"
"It does share your IP-address with the server but that is required for "
"internet to function.\n"
"\n"
"The list of known issues it downloads is rarely used, mostly the issues are "
"at [jasp-issues](https://github.com/jasp-stats/jasp-issues/issues). \n"
"However if we realize a terrible error has slipped into an analysis this "
"will show you *in the analysis* that there is something you should take into "
"account. \n"
"Luckily we almost never need to use it.\n"
"\n"
"### Zoom\n"
"This number specifies how the JASP interface will be scaled.\n"
"All menus and results are scaled by this factor.\n"
"You can also use keyboard shortcuts instead:\n"
"\n"
"- OSX:  &#8984; with `+`, `-` or `0`\n"
"- Windows and Linux: Ctrl with `+`, `-` or `0`\n"
"\n"
"`-` decreases and `+` increases the scale while `0` resets the scaling to "
"its default value.\n"
"\n"
"### Scroll speed\n"
"This speed determines, in pixels per second, what the maximum flick / scroll "
"speed is of certain moving elements in JASP.\n"
"Should you find that scrolling in the options goes too fast, or too slow, "
"you can change this.\n"
"\n"
"### Safe Graphics\n"
"If this is enabled JASP will run in software rendering mode, which means "
"your interface is slower but weird glitches or other problems might "
"disappear. You will need to restart JASP for this option to take effect "
"though!\n"
"\n"
"### Use Native File Dialogs\n"
"Some users on certain systems (Windows) can run into trouble with the "
"default native (or system) file dialogs.\n"
"When they try to open or save a file in JASP by clicking \"Browse\" in one "
"of the file menus JASP crashes.\n"
"If you disable this option we use Qt file dialogs and they might not crash. "
"Most users will not need this option disabled though.\n"
msgstr ""
"\n\nInterfaccia utente\n=========\n\nCon i parametri dell'interfaccia"
"utente in JASP è possibile specificare le seguenti opzioni:\n(Tutte queste"
"impostazioni rimarranno dopo il riavvio JASP.)\n\n### Caratteri\nQui è"
"possibile specificare quale carattere verrà utilizzato dall'interfaccia"
"(Menu File, barra Ribbon, pannello Opzioni analisi), il R, Lavaan o JAGS"
"Code oppure i risultati. Se non viene effettuata alcuna scelta, viene"
"utilizzato il carattere predefinito.\n\nUtilizza il rendering del testo"
"di Qt: JASP utilizzando Qt per eseguire il rendering del testo"
"dell'interfaccia per impostazione predefinita. JASP utilizzerà il rendering"
"del testo nativo quando l'opzione è deselezionata. Prova a"
"selezionare/deselezionare questa opzione se trovi che il testo su JASP"
"sembra terribile (sfocato o sfocato...). Ciò avrà effetto dopo il riavvio"
"di JASP.\n\n### Temi\nQui puoi specificare se desideri avere un tema"
"\"chiaro\" o \"scuro\" sull'interfaccia di JASP. Il tema scuro è molto più"
"gradevole alla vista se ti trovi in un ambiente buio, mentre il tema chiaro"
"(predefinito) è più chiaro in condizioni di luce intensa.\n\n### Lingua"
"preferita\nSeleziona la lingua in cui desideri utilizzare JASP.\n\nSe"
"desideri migliorare le nostre traduzioni o aggiungere una nuova lingua,"
"visita https://jasp-stats.org/translation-guidelines/\n\n### Impostazioni"
"internazionali alternative\nQuesta è per impostazione predefinita attivo e"
"abilita un menu di selezione di lingue e territori. \nQualunque cosa"
"selezioni qui determinerà come saranno formattati i tuoi numeri in JASP, ad"
"esempio, ma non limitato a, se il separatore decimale è un ',' o un '.' ad"
"esempio.\nVerrà utilizzato anche per formattare le colonne monetarie"
"nell'output e, in futuro, anche le colonne di data, ora e dataora."
"\nDeterminerà anche il tipo di separatori delle migliaia da"
"utilizzare.\nSe disabiliti l'utilizzo di impostazioni locali alternative,"
"verranno utilizzate le impostazioni internazionali corrispondenti alla"
"lingua selezionata.\n\n### Modalità di navigazione ALT\nAttiva o"
"disattiva la modalità di navigazione attivata da ALT.\n\n### Controlla"
"aggiornamenti\nJASP non condivide nessuno dei tuoi dati quando riceve"
"aggiornamenti, nemmeno la versione di JASP che stai"
"utilizzando.\nCondivide il tuo indirizzo IP con il server ma è necessario"
"per il funzionamento di Internet.\n\nL'elenco dei problemi noti che"
"scarica viene utilizzato raramente, la maggior parte dei problemi si trova"
"su [jasp-issues](https://github.com/jasp-stats/jasp-issues/issues)."
"\nTuttavia, se ci rendiamo conto che un terribile errore è scivolato in"
"un'analisi, questo ti mostrerà *nell'analisi* che c'è qualcosa di cui"
"dovresti tenere conto. \nFortunatamente non abbiamo quasi mai bisogno di"
"usarlo.\n\n### Zoom\nQuesto numero specifica come verrà ridimensionata"
"l'interfaccia JASP.\nTutti i menu e i risultati vengono ridimensionati in"
"base a questo fattore.\nPuoi anche utilizzare le scorciatoie da"
"tastiera:\n\n- OSX: &#8984; con `+`, `-` o `0`\n- Windows e Linux: Ctrl"
"con `+`, `-` o `0`\n\n`-` diminuisce e `+` aumenta la scala mentre \"0\""
"ripristina la scala al suo valore predefinito.\n\n### Velocità di"
"scorrimento\nQuesta velocità determina, in pixel al secondo, qual è la"
"velocità massima di scorrimento/scorrimento di determinati elementi in"
"movimento in JASP.\nSe scopri che lo scorrimento nelle opzioni è troppo"
"veloce o troppo lento, puoi modificarlo.\n\n### Grafica sicura\nSe"
"questa opzione è abilitata, JASP verrà eseguito in modalità rendering"
"software, il che significa che l'interfaccia sarà più lenta ma strani"
"difetti o altri problemi potrebbero scomparire. Dovrai riavviare JASP"
"perché questa opzione abbia effetto!\n\n### Usa finestre di dialogo file"
"nativi\nAlcuni utenti su determinati sistemi (Windows) possono avere"
"problemi con le finestre di dialogo file native (o di sistema)"
"predefinite.\nQuando tentano di aprire o salvare un file in JASP facendo"
"clic su \"Sfoglia\" in uno dei menu file JASP si blocca.\nSe disattivi"
"questa opzione opzione usiamo le finestre di dialogo dei file Qt e"
"potrebbero non bloccarsi. Tuttavia, per la maggior parte degli utenti non"
"sarà necessario disabilitare questa opzione.\n"

msgctxt "AllHelp|"
msgid ""
"\n"
"<h1>Computed Columns</h1>\n"
"\n"
"<details>\n"
"\t<summary><b>Adding a Computed Column</b></summary>\n"
"\t<img src=\"other/CreatingComputedColumn.gif\"/>\n"
"\t<p>When you have the dataview of JASP in front of you then in the top-"
"right corner there should be a '+'-symbol.\n"
"\t\tWhen you click this it opens a dialog that lets you create a computed "
"column, you are required to enter a valid and unused column name for it.</"
"p>\n"
"\t<p>\tYou can choose whether you want to define it through R code or "
"through the drag &amp; drop constructor, similar to those used in filtering."
"</p>\n"
"\t<p>\tThe initial type for your column can be selected as well. This can be "
"changed at any later point anyway, just like the other columns (through the "
"type dropdown menu from the columnheader icon). </p>\n"
"\t<p> You can also change any column to a computed column at any time via "
"the variables window.\n"
"\t\t Or change it from a drag&amp;drop to a r-code computed column, it will "
"give you the r-code of any drag&amp;drop columns, but not the other way "
"round.</p>\n"
"</details>\n"
"\n"
"<details>\n"
"\t<summary><b>Using Drag &amp; Drop Constructor</b></summary>\n"
"\t<p>Computing new data using the Drag &amp; Drop constructor is quite "
"straight-forward, especially if you've already used it for filtering. Still, "
"some examples follow:</p>\n"
"\t<ul>\n"
"\t\t<li>\n"
"\t\t\t<b>Converting a Scale (Height Ratio -> Height)</b>\n"
"\t\t\t<img src=\"other/ComputedColumnHeight.gif\"/>\n"
"\t\t\t<p>Supposing we have a column <i>Height Ratio</i> and we want to "
"convert it back to proper heights. We assume that 180cm is the norm and with "
"this knowledge the animation above should be clear.</p> \n"
"\t\t</li>\n"
"\t\t<li>\n"
"\t\t\t\t<b>Converting to Logical (as text)</b>\n"
"\t\t\t\t<img src=\"other/ComputedColumnPopularVote.gif\"/>\n"
"\t\t\t\t<p>Supposing we have a column <i>Popular Vote</i> and we want to "
"show clearly who got it and who didn't. We create a column with <i>Nominal "
"Text</i> as type and specify the requested formula, see above animation for "
"details.</p> \n"
"\t\t\t</li>\n"
"\t\t\t<li>\n"
"\t\t\t\t<b>Dividing a scalar (Height Ratio) into 3 named fields</b>\n"
"\t\t\t\t<img src=\"other/ComputedColumnHeightCategoryDragNDrop.gif\"/>\n"
"\t\t\t\t<p>Supposing we have a column <i>Height Ratio</i> and we want divide "
"the rows up based on if they are <i>Short</i> or <i>Tall</i> or not. We "
"create a column with <i>Nominal Text</i> as type and use the <i>cut</i> "
"function to divide <i>Height Ratio</i> into 3 levels. Afterwards we give "
"each of the intervals a nice name to clarify what's what.</p> \n"
"\t\t\t</li>\n"
"\t</ul>\n"
"\t<p>A new feature missing from the rather old help gifs is the changing of "
"a columntype within the constructor. Just click the icon and choose the type "
"you want, the tooltip will show an example of the data.</p>\n"
"</details>\n"
"\n"
"<details>\n"
"\t<summary><b>Using R</b></summary>\n"
"\t<p>Computing new data using R is quite straightforward, you can use many "
"standard functions and can refer to other columns through name.</p>\n"
"\t<p>Some examples:\n"
"\t\t\t<ul>\n"
"\t\t\t\t<li><p><b>Multiplying a scalar (Height Ratio) by some fixed value</"
"b></p>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<p>Make sure that your column is defined as <i>Scale</i>, even "
"though the other types would also work.</p>\n"
"\t\t\t\t<p class=\"code\">Height Ratio * 180</p>\n"
"\t\t\t</li>\n"
"\t\t\t\t<li><p><b>Dividing a scalar (Height Ratio) into 3 named fields</b></"
"p>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<p>Make sure that your column is defined as <i>Nominal Text</i></"
"p>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"code\">cut(Height Ratio, 3, c(\"Short\", \"Average\", "
"\"Tall\"))</p>\n"
"\t\t\t\t</li>\n"
"\t\t\t</ul>\n"
"\t\t\tThere are of course many possibilities and you are only limited by the "
"availability of (safe) functions in JASP. Should you miss something than "
"don't hesitate to contact us so we may add it to the next version.\n"
"\t</p>\n"
"\t<p>You can also transform the type of the column by appending the wanted "
"type. So suppose we have a column <code>chestnut</code> we can convert it to "
"any type as: <code>chestnut.scale, chestnut.ordinal and chestnut.nominal</"
"code>. </p>\n"
"</details>\n"
"\n"
"<details>\n"
"\t<summary><b>Rowwise operations</b></summary>\n"
"\t<p>\n"
"\t\tA special type of computed column creation is the rowwise operations of "
"mean, variance etc.<br><br>\n"
"\t\tThese functions are:<br>\n"
"\t\t<code>rowMean, rowSum, rowSD, rowVariance, rowMedian, rowMin, rowMax</"
"code><br>\n"
"\t\tThey will probably return NA for when any of the column's rows contain "
"one.\n"
"\t\tSo there are also variants like <code>rowMeanNaRm</code> available that "
"drop NA first.<br><br>\n"
"\t\tIt can be used like: <code>rowMean(Column 1, Column 2, Column 3)</code>\n"
"\t</p>\n"
"</details>\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""
